{"hash":"11996d69fa7f6275","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":90.92,"said":"Look up at this great bell, hanging in its stone frame before the temple. Now look closely at its face. You may see a cross, and the date 1729. This bell was cast for a church. In 1739 the Maratha army, led by Chimaji Appa, the Peshwa’s younger brother, took the sea fort of Vasai, near Mumbai, from the Portuguese after a long siege. Chimaji brought great bells back from Vasai and gave them to temples, and this one he offered to Bhimashankar. Ring it, and the whole hollow answers. The temple itself is older. A shrine here is mentioned in writings as early as the thirteenth century, and the trust says the Maratha kings, from Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj on, gave for its worship. Then, in the eighteenth century, came Nana Phadnavis, the shrewd minister who held the Peshwas’ government together in Pune. He built the shikhara you see rising over the sanctum, and the old pillared hall, giving this black-stone temple much of the shape it has today. A church bell, a minister’s tower, a king’s gift: everything here has been offered to the Lord, one way or another. Now climb the steps into the hall.\n\nDid you know? The trust says the bell weighs five maunds, roughly two hundred kilograms. Other Vasai bells hang in temples across Maharashtra.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1105},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1121,"end":1249}],"words":[[0.098,0.228,0,4],[0.338,0.192,5,7],[0.543,0.12,8,10],[0.675,0.168,11,15],[0.855,0.385,16,21],[1.252,0.373,22,26],[1.877,0.421,28,35],[2.31,0.096,36,38],[2.418,0.18,39,42],[2.61,0.349,43,48],[2.971,0.349,49,54],[3.331,0.325,55,61],[3.668,0.096,62,65],[3.776,0.529,66,72],[5.192,0.228,74,77],[5.433,0.168,78,82],[5.613,0.505,83,90],[6.13,0.156,91,93],[6.298,0.18,94,97],[6.49,0.445,98,102],[7.824,0.156,104,107],[7.993,0.192,108,111],[8.197,0.192,112,115],[8.401,0.048,116,117],[8.462,0.529,118,123],[9.207,0.144,125,128],[9.363,0.084,129,132],[9